[" Below is a high-level design and pseudo-code for the `DynamicLogManager` asynchronous logging framework, adhering to the given requirements. Note that this is not production-ready code but provides an outline for implementation.

```typescript
// Import required dependencies (e.g., message broker client, JSON library)
import { MessageBrokerClient } from 'message-broker';
import * as json from 'json';
import { Cache } from 'cache';

class DynamicLogManager {
  private readonly messageBroker: MessageBrokerClient;
  private readonly cache: Cache;
  private readonly backpressureThreshold: number; // Adjust this according to your system's throughput and latency requirements

  constructor() {
    this.messageBroker = new MessageBrokerClient('logging-broker');
    this.cache = new Cache({ evictionPolicy: 'LRU' });
    this.backpressureThreshold = 100; // Adjust as needed
  }

  // Define the log function, which handles structured logging and rate limiting
  log(serviceName: string, level: string, message: string, errorDetails?: any) {
    const timestamp = new Date().toISOString();
    const logData = { service: serviceName, timestamp, level, message, details: errorDetails };

    // Validate the log data against a predefined schema
    if (!validateLogData(logData)) {
      console.error('Invalid log data', logData);
      return;
    }

    // Rate limit error submissions per service and minute
    const key = `${serviceName}:errors`;
    if (this.cache.has(key) && this.cache.get(key).length >= 50) {
      console.warn(`Rate limiting: ${serviceName} has exceeded the error submission limit of 50 per minute`);
      return;
    }

    // Add the log data to the local cache for rate-limiting purposes
    this.cache.set(key, [logData]);

    // Serialize the log data as JSON and send it asynchronously to the message broker
    const serializedLog = json.stringify(logData);
    this.messageBroker.send('logging-queue', serializedLog)
      .then(() => console.debug(`Logged: ${logData}`))
      .catch((error) => {
        console.error(`Error logging data: ${logData}`, error);
        // Handle connectivity issues, retry logic, and fallback strategies here
      });
  }

  // Implement backpressure mechanism to throttle incoming log events when the external service is saturated
  private handleBackpressure() {
    if (this.messageBroker.queuedMessages.length >= this.backpressureThreshold) {
      console.warn('Backpressure detected, pausing log submissions temporarily');
      setTimeout(() => this.handleBackpressure(), 100); // Adjust the delay according to your system's requirements
    }
  }
}
```

This pseudo-code covers the core functionality of the `DynamicLogManager`. You'll need to handle the retry mechanism, fallback strategies, and error handling as per the given requirements. Properly profile the logger's performance to ensure it meets your system's latency and throughput requirements. Additionally, you should refine the design for better thread safety, performance optimization, and maintainability."]

