[' Here's a high-level structure for your command-line utility using Tokio, Rust's async/await model, and a simple in-memory cache for DNS resolution results. This implementation serves as a starting point to build the robust tool you described, incorporating necessary optimizations, error handling, and testability.

```rust
use std::env;
use tokio::sync::RwLock;
use tokio::{task, run_blocking};
use futures::{StreamExt, FutureExt};
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use reqwest::Client;

#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct Config {
    sources: Vec<Source>,
}

#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
enum Source {
    Local(Vec<String>),
    Remote(String),
}

async fn resolve_dns(domain: &str, cache: &mut RwLock<DnsCache>) -> Option<String> {
    if let Some(ip) = cache.read().await.get(domain) {
        return Some(ip);
    }

    // Perform DNS query and store the result in the cache for future lookups.
    let ip = // Implement your preferred method for resolving DNS queries, e.g., using `tokio-dns` or `reqwest`.

    cache.write().await.insert(domain, ip);
    Some(ip)
}

struct DnsCache {
    inner: std::collections::HashMap<String, String>,
}

impl DnsCache {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            inner: std::collections::HashMap::new(),
        }
    }
}

async fn process_domains(domains: &[String], cache: &mut RwLock<DnsCache>) {
    let mut tasks = Vec::with_capacity(domains.len());

    for domain in domains {
        let cache = cache.clone();
        tasks.push(resolve_dns(domain, &cache).boxed())
    }

    // Use Tokio's `join_all` method to execute all tasks concurrently and collect their results.
    let results: Vec<Option<String>> = futures::future::join_all(tasks).await;

    for (i, result) in results.into_iter().enumerate() {
        println!("{} -> {}", domains[i], result.unwrap_or("<unresolved>"));
    }
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let args: Vec<String> = env::args().collect();
    if args.len() < 2 {
        eprintln!("Usage: {} [CONFIG_FILE]", args[0]);
        return;
    }

    let config_path = &args[1];
    let config: Config = toml::from_str(&std::fs::read_to_string(config_path).expect("Failed to read configuration file")).unwrap();

    // Initialize the DNS cache.
    let mut dns_cache = RwLock::new(DnsCache::new());

    match config.sources[0] {
        Source::Local(domains) => process_domains(&domains, &mut dns_cache).await,
        Source::Remote(url) => {
            let client = Client::new();
            let resp = client.get(url).send().await.unwrap();
            let body = resp.text().await.unwrap();
            // Parse the remote domain list and process it asynchronously.
        }
    }
}
```

This code provides a basic structure for your command-line utility, handling DNS queries from a configuration file and using an in-memory cache to store resolved IP addresses. You'll need to add error handling, caching optimization, scalability improvements, and other features according to the requirements you listed. Additionally, consider writing unit tests and integration tests to ensure reliable operation under various conditions.

To specify the configuration file, simply provide the path to a TOML-formatted file containing one or more domain lists (either local or remote). For example:

```toml
[[sources]]
type = "local"
domains = [
    "google.com",
