[' Here is a basic implementation of `DOMFilter` module in JavaScript:

```javascript
class DOMFilter {
  constructor(root) {
    this.root = root;
    this.cache = new Map();
    this.concurrencyLimit = 5; // Adjust as needed for optimal performance
  }

  async selectElements(selectors, callback) {
    const processedNodes = new Set();

    async function processNode(node) {
      if (processedNodes.has(node)) return;
      processedNodes.add(node);

      for (const selector of selectors) {
        try {
          const result = await selector(node);
          if (result) {
            callback(node);
            break;
          }
        } catch (error) {
          console.error(`Error evaluating selector on node ${node}:`, error);
        }
      }

      Array.from(node.childNodes).forEach(child => processNode(child));
    }

    const promises = [];

    for (const child of this.root.children) {
      if (!processedNodes.has(child)) {
        promises.push(processNode(child));
      }
    }

    await Promise.all(promises);
  }

  // Caching mechanism
  cacheResult(key, value) {
    this.cache.set(key, value);
  }

  getCachedResult(key) {
    return this.cache.get(key);
  }
}
```

To use the `DOMFilter`, first create an instance of it with a root DOM node:

```javascript
const myFilter = new DOMFilter(document.body);
```

Then, define your selector functions. These functions should return a promise that resolves to either true (if the element meets the criteria) or false. You can use async/await for simplicity, but feel free to implement custom asynchronous logic as needed:

```javascript
