['```java
@Component
public class PaymentProcessingService {

    private final Map<String, ThirdPartyApi> thirdPartyApis;
    private final Cache<Long, Transaction> transactionCache;
    private final MessagingSystem messagingSystem;
    private final EncryptionService encryptionService;

    public PaymentProcessingService(
            @Qualifier("thirdPartyApis") Map<String, ThirdPartyApi> thirdPartyApis,
            Cache<Long, Transaction> transactionCache,
            MessagingSystem messagingSystem,
            EncryptionService encryptionService) {
        this.thirdPartyApis = thirdPartyApis;
        this.transactionCache = transactionCache;
        this.messagingSystem = messagingSystem;
        this.encryptionService = encryptionService;
    }

    public void processPayment(Transaction transaction) {
        // Validate and encrypt sensitive information
        transaction = encryptionService.encrypt(transaction);

        // Store in distributed cache for fast retrieval
        transactionCache.put(transaction.getId(), transaction);

        // Decouple validation and fraud detection processes
        messagingSystem.sendValidationRequest(transaction);
        messagingSystem.sendFraudDetectionRequest(transaction);

        // Handle one-time charges, subscription-based payments, and refunds
        switch (transaction.getType()) {
            case ONE_TIME:
                handleOneTimePayment(transaction);
                break;
            case SUBSCRIPTION:
                handleSubscriptionPayment(transaction);
                break;
            case REFUND:
                handleRefund(transaction);
                break;
        }
    }

    // ... (methods for handling one-time payments, subscription-based payments, and refunds)
}
```

This code demonstrates a basic PaymentProcessingService that processes cross-border credit card transactions. It uses a map of ThirdPartyApi instances to interact with multiple external financial APIs, ensuring seamless integration with existing billing systems. The service stores transactions in a distributed cache for fast retrieval and decouples transaction validation and fraud detection using a messaging system.

The service employs adaptive rate limiting and backpressure strategies by sending requests asynchronously to external providers. It also encrypts sensitive information both in transit and at rest, ensuring secure data handling and compliance with PCI-DSS standards.

For implementing low-latency and high-throughput operations, consider using non-blocking I/O operations, efficient data structures, and caching strategies. To support easy integration of new payment gateways and transaction types, design the architecture to be flexible and backward compatible.

Deployment strategy should account for different geographic regions and regulatory requirements by configuring the service based on specific regional needs. Implement comprehensive monitoring and alerting for critical events like failed transactions, fraudulent activities, and system errors.

For handling concurrency during peak hours, consider using load balancing techniques such as round-robin or least connections, and ensure that caching strategies are optimized to minimize latency. For addressing potential API throttling from external providers, implement adaptive rate limiting and backpressure strategies, allowing the service to adjust its request rates based on the response from the third-party APIs.

Finally, ensure that the service is monitored and maintained regularly for optimal performance and security compliance across diverse environments.']

