   Here's an example of how to implement the `Parser` struct and its related functions:

```rust
// GrammarToLLVM/src/parsing.rs
use std::fs::{File, OpenOptions};
use std::io::{BufReader, BufWriter};
use std::path::Path;
use std::sync::Arc;
use tempfile::NamedTempFile;
use serde_yaml as yaml;
use grammar::Grammar;
use caching::Cache;

pub struct Parser {
    grammar: Arc<Grammar>,
    cache: Cache,
}

impl Parser {
    pub fn new(data_source: external_data::DataSource) -> Self {
        let grammar = load_grammar(&data_source);
        let cache = Cache::new();
        Parser { grammar, cache }
    }

    pub fn run(&mut self) {
        // Load and parse input files using the specified grammar
        for input in get_input_files() {
            match self.parse_file(input) {
                Ok(llvm_ir) => self.cache.save(input, llvm_ir),
                Err(e) => log::error!("Error parsing {}: {}", input.display(), e),
            }
        }
    }

    fn parse_file(&mut self, path: &Path) -> Result<String, Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
        let mut file = File::open(path)?;
        let reader = BufReader::new(file);
        let grammar_ast = yaml::from_reader(reader)?;
        self.grammar.parse(grammar_ast).map_err(|e| e.into())
    }
}
```

   The above code outline demonstrates the high-level structure of the `GrammarToLLVM` library and provides an example implementation for the `Parser` struct. You'll need to fill in the details, including the actual implementation of the `grammar`, `external_data`, `caching`, `performance`, `integration`, and other modules, as well as the `load_config()`, `get_input_files()`, and other helper functions. Don't forget to add appropriate unit and integration tests for each module!']

