Here is a minimal example of the `FileSystemTraversalService` class:

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class FileSystemTraversalService {
    private final List<String> excludedDirectories;
    // ... other fields and constructors

    public void traverseDirectory(Path directory, BlockingQueue<Runnable> taskQueue) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        if (Files.isSymbolicLink(directory)) {
            Path resolvedPath = Files.readSymbolicLink(directory);
            traverseDirectory(resolvedPath, taskQueue);
            return;
        }

        try (Stream<Path> paths = Files.walk(directory, 1)) {
            List<Path> files = paths.filter(Files::isRegularFile)
                    .filter(path -> !excludedDirectories.contains(path.getFileName().toString()))
                    .collect(Collectors.toList());

            for (Path file : files) {
                if (Files.isHidden(file)) {
                    taskQueue.add(() -> handleFile(file));
                }
            }

            List<Path> directories = paths.filter(Files::isDirectory)
                    .filter(path -> !excludedDirectories.contains(path.getFileName().toString()))
                    .collect(Collectors.toList());

            for (Path dir : directories) {
                taskQueue.add(() -> traverseDirectory(dir, taskQueue));
            }
        }
    }

    // ... other methods for handling files and errors
}
```

This is a starting point for the application. Each module should be developed separately and tested thoroughly before integrating with others. Additionally, consider using popular Java libraries such as Spring for dependency injection or Guava for concurrency utilities to leverage existing solutions and best practices.']

